\begin{definition}\label{def:ElGamalsig}\index{digital signature!ElGamal}
Suppose Alice has ElGamal private key $(p,r,\alpha)$ and wishes to digitally
sign the message $m\in\Mm$.  She first chooses a random $\gamma\in\NN$
satisfying $1<\gamma<p-1$ and
$\gcd(\gamma,p-1)=1$.\index{greatest common divisor!used}

The digital signature on $m$ is
$(r^\gamma\pmod*{p},\gamma^{-1}\cdot(m-\alpha r^\gamma)\pmod*{p-1})$, where the
inverse is taken in mod $p-1$.

To verify the signature $(x,y)$ on message $m$ using Alice's public key
$(p,r,a)$, Bob checks to see if $a^x x^y\equiv r^m\pmod{p}$: if so, he accepts;
if not, he rejects.
\end{definition}

